# DMESG(1)

## NAME

dmesg - print the kernel message ring

## SYNOPSIS

```c
dmesg
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Dmesg* prints the contents of the kernel's message ring, oldest character first.

Every character of every diagnostic the kernel prints goes to the operator's console and, on
the way, into a circular buffer of **MSGBUFS** characters (*putchar*() in **kernel/dev/sc.c**).
The buffer wraps and keeps no mark of where it did, so what *dmesg* prints is the last
**MSGBUFS** characters the kernel produced, which normally begins part way through a line.
Carriage returns are not stored and neither are nulls, so a null in the buffer is a character
position the kernel has never yet reached; those are skipped.

*Dmesg* takes no arguments and needs no privilege. It reads the buffer and the cursor with two
kctl(2) calls and opens no device.

## SEE ALSO

kctl(2), ps(1), pstat(1M)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Dmesg* complains if this kernel exports no **msgbuf** or **msgbufp**, or if the cursor does
not point inside the buffer, and exits 1. The last of those means the table in
**kernel/ksym.c** is wrong; it cannot mean what v7's "Namelist mismatch" meant, since there is
no **/unix** on this system for a namelist to be stale against.

## BUGS

**Note: this page is new.** *Dmesg* is one of the two commands in the v7 **/usr/src/cmd** tree
that came with no manual page anywhere in the system; the other is yes.

**Note:** v7's *dmesg* kept a copy of the buffer in **/usr/adm/msgbuf** and printed only what
had appeared since the last run, with a date line above it and **...** where the two copies
diverged; its **-** flag suppressed the update of that file. Neither the file nor the directory
is on this system and all of it is gone, the date line included. This *dmesg* prints the whole
ring every time.
